1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
Pfft!

2
00:00:02,919 --> 00:00:04,080
The Stapler

3
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:10,200
Tom Thomas gets the ball!

4
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:12,640
He makes an incredible move!

5
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:14,359
He's wide open!

6
00:00:19,199 --> 00:00:22,080
The goalie sees him and he screams in horror!

7
00:00:23,879 --> 00:00:25,120
He shoots and…

8
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:27,920
scores!

9
00:00:28,199 --> 00:00:29,120
Tideesh!

10
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:31,519
Tom Thomas, stop kicking that ball!

11
00:00:31,679 --> 00:00:36,079
Your school concert starts in 30 minutes and I don't want to iron your pants again!

12
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:37,039
Alright, Mom.

13
00:00:40,799 --> 00:00:42,439
Just one more time, huh?

14
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:46,400
No, I'm sorry. Mom said I have to quit kicking the ball.

15
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:51,320
But Mom said nothing about dribbling the ball!

16
00:00:51,880 --> 00:00:54,719
Go Tom Thomas, go!

17
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:04,000
Whoa!

18
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:15,900
Tom Thomas! Your pants!

19
00:01:17,100 --> 00:01:21,000
This can't be! These are my special concert pants!

20
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:25,000
Yeah? How will you go now?

21
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:27,500
Well, your mom does have enough time to sew them.

22
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:28,700
I'm sorry.

23
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:30,800
I'm scared to even tell her about it.

24
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:32,920
She said that I had to stop playing.

25
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:35,920
Hey! I've got it!

26
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:39,400
Here!

27
00:01:40,040 --> 00:01:43,240
You think we should fix the rip in his pants with a stapler?

28
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:45,159
Yeah! Isn't it a good idea?

29
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:47,600
Huh? I gotta try it out.

30
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:48,920
You do.

31
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:52,000
Like this?

32
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:53,640
Stop!

33
00:01:53,719 --> 00:01:54,840
Why? What's wrong?

34
00:01:55,640 --> 00:01:56,920
Eh, my nose itches.

35
00:01:57,040 --> 00:01:58,200
That's all. Let's go!

36
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:02,200
You're right, Nolik, it works!

37
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:03,760
That is super!

38
00:02:04,480 --> 00:02:07,280
Yeah! The stapler is really great!

39
00:02:07,719 --> 00:02:09,199
Do you guys know how it works?

40
00:02:09,199 --> 00:02:10,960
Just keep stapling and I'll tell you.

41
00:02:12,879 --> 00:02:16,000
The staples for a stapler are lightly glued together.

42
00:02:16,120 --> 00:02:20,039
That way you can load many staples at once instead of one at a time.

43
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:23,199
And a spring pushes the staples to the front.

44
00:02:23,199 --> 00:02:29,000
When you push down on the arm, a metal tooth pushes the front staple down through a thin space,

45
00:02:29,199 --> 00:02:31,579
and the staple punches holes in the paper.

46
00:02:31,979 --> 00:02:35,399
Next, the pointy ends of the staple push down onto a plate,

47
00:02:35,659 --> 00:02:38,459
and that makes the staple bend behind the paper.

48
00:02:38,819 --> 00:02:41,280
And there you go! The papers are fastened!

49
00:02:43,879 --> 00:02:48,659
So you could say that we're sewing, but using a stapler instead of a needle.

50
00:02:49,439 --> 00:02:51,219
Yeah, and it works even faster.

51
00:02:52,060 --> 00:02:52,659
Huh?

52
00:02:53,060 --> 00:02:54,180
What's going on?

53
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:56,620
Could it have run out of staples?

54
00:03:01,939 --> 00:03:03,939
There's still a lot more staples,

55
00:03:04,180 --> 00:03:07,099
but one of them got jammed here in the slot!

56
00:03:11,379 --> 00:03:13,860
Tom Thomas, we're leaving in five minutes!

57
00:03:13,939 --> 00:03:14,900
OK, Mom!

58
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:16,379
So, did you get it?

59
00:03:16,579 --> 00:03:17,460
No.

60
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:20,900
Why don't we get Papus to help us?

61
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:24,300
Because he's really strong and he's got a pack-o-mat.

62
00:03:25,099 --> 00:03:26,740
We can do this ourselves.

63
00:03:26,860 --> 00:03:30,340
Tom Thomas, find something we can use to push that staple out.

64
00:03:35,140 --> 00:03:38,099
The stapler is not a very new invention.

65
00:03:38,580 --> 00:03:42,300
It's been said that the French king, King Louis XV,

66
00:03:42,379 --> 00:03:46,060
had a stapler made out of gold and precious gems.

67
00:03:46,180 --> 00:03:49,939
Unfortunately, it could only hold one staple at a time.

68
00:03:49,939 --> 00:03:52,419
Modern staplers are much more convenient,

69
00:03:52,419 --> 00:03:54,900
and people have come up with so many kinds.

70
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:57,859
For paper, for plywood, and even for skin!

71
00:03:57,859 --> 00:04:01,340
Yes, surgeons often use them during operations.

72
00:04:01,340 --> 00:04:05,259
Then there's the staple gun that's used to upholster furniture.

73
00:04:05,259 --> 00:04:07,300
And its older brother, the nail gun,

74
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:11,060
can even be used to hold together the walls of a building.

75
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:14,900
And here's an invention almost as important as a stapler.

76
00:04:14,900 --> 00:04:17,060
It's the staple remover!

77
00:04:17,060 --> 00:04:21,939
With its help it's possible to remove the staples put in by a stapler.

78
00:04:26,339 --> 00:04:27,939
How about this screwdriver?

79
00:04:27,939 --> 00:04:28,899
That'll work!

80
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:33,300
Look, the screwdriver fits perfectly into the slot.

81
00:04:33,420 --> 00:04:34,540
Ah, that's great!

82
00:04:34,540 --> 00:04:37,980
Now push that staple through, only keep your fingers out of the way.

83
00:04:37,980 --> 00:04:39,579
Or you won't finish sewing.

84
00:04:40,459 --> 00:04:41,420
Tideesh!

85
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:43,139
It's still not tideesh.

86
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:45,300
You haven't fixed your pants yet.

87
00:04:46,300 --> 00:04:48,300
That's it! They're done!

88
00:04:52,300 --> 00:04:53,300
Now we can say,

89
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:54,300
Tideesh!

90
00:04:55,300 --> 00:04:57,300
Tom Thomas, are you ready?

91
00:04:57,300 --> 00:04:58,300
Of course!

92
00:05:01,300 --> 00:05:04,300
My idea with the stapler was smart, wasn't it?

93
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:06,300
And Tom Thomas' mother won't notice a thing.

94
00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:07,300
Will too.

95
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:09,300
Just wait till she washes them.

